WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.449
Film Courage: I think this might be part of
your new book [A SURVIVOR’S GUIDE TO HOLLYWOOD:

00:00:02.449 --> 00:00:04.490
How To Play The Game Without Losing Your Soul]
but this is something that you talked about

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:08.390
in terms of getting self-absorbed in Hollywood?

00:00:08.390 --> 00:00:09.390
What is it?

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:11.990
What does it look like, so we can define it?

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:19.089
Robin Riker: Well I think being self-absorbed
looks pretty much the same in every town and

00:00:19.089 --> 00:00:25.199
country across the globe.

00:00:25.199 --> 00:00:31.420
I see it in other people when they just don’t…you
know, sometimes you’re walking down the

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:34.070
street and someone goes “Hi!”

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:39.440
And you are absent-minded or thinking about
something else and you respond “Just fine!”

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:40.440
No.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.590
That isn’t what they said!

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:43.590
But you didn’t hear that.

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:44.590
You were in your own mind.

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:51.690
And there are lot of people in this town,
in everywhere, that are in there own heads

00:00:51.690 --> 00:01:02.120
and they don’t ever ask about you, and they
are not good ball players.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:05.030
In conversations, you pass the ball.

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:08.030
And if you don’t pass the ball then you
are having a monologue.

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:11.289
It’s not really that interesting to people.

00:01:11.289 --> 00:01:15.100
And that’s what it looks like to me.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:18.869
There is this one actress who I encounter
all the time.

00:01:18.869 --> 00:01:24.329
She absolutely knows who I am but every time
she meets me it’s the first time.

00:01:24.329 --> 00:01:31.780
And we have gone up against each other for
roles and I’ve gotten some of them and she’s

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:35.549
gotten some of them.

00:01:35.549 --> 00:01:39.420
I think that a lot of the people who are the
most self-absorbed are also those who are

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:47.520
the most threatened, who feel that if they
are not focused on themselves and what it

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:51.399
is they need and what it is they want and
what it is that perhaps you can deliver to

00:01:51.399 --> 00:01:58.279
them, then they are not taking care of business.

00:01:58.279 --> 00:02:04.000
They have to be…it’s all about the next
thing.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.090
And that sort of reminds me of this man.

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:13.380
When I got my first television show, I was
going to celebrate and I took myself out to

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:16.710
this place called Joe Allen's (it’s called
something else now and it’s quite a popular

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:19.000
place but I don’t remember).

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:25.080
My father was an actor and my parents divorced
when I was very young and he was a fantastic

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:33.120
actor and even though I’ve written and acted
since I could write or speak, I think I pursued

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:39.350
acting in a way to kind of bring my father
closer to me (at least metaphysically, psychologically).

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:41.510
And so he was the first one I called.

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:44.880
My mother had supported me my whole life,
but my father was the one I wanted to call

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:47.570
and say that I got my first job in TV.

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:51.150
So I went to this bar and called him from
a pay phone (do you remember what those are,

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:54.760
any of you out there?).

00:02:54.760 --> 00:03:01.020
And I told him about it and he wasn’t that
excited for me because he was a little jealous

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:05.400
himself because he was self-absorbed.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:11.010
And instead of being joyful for his daughter,
even though he was my father and we wouldn’t

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:15.060
ever go up for the same parts, I had landed
something that was really good.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:18.590
And he couldn’t really support me in that.

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:27.010
And my spirit really felt a wound, I played
it off because that’s how I roll, but it

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:28.160
hurt me.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:32.810
But I shook it right off and said “He is
not going to crunch my buzz.

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:34.950
I am going to carry on with this celebration.”

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:37.260
So I went to the bar and I bought myself a
cocktail.

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:44.660
And I was chatting with the bartender and
then a man came in and I struck up a conversation.

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:52.470
And I was facing him and he was facing the
door and he was talking to me, but I could

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:55.690
see that he was always looking at the door,
he was always waiting, every time the door

00:03:55.690 --> 00:03:56.830
opened.

00:03:56.830 --> 00:03:58.260
He was looking, because, you know, I was “nobody.”

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:07.470
I was just some girl at the bar and he was
waiting for somebody more important than I

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:09.819
or somebody who could do something important
for him.

00:04:09.819 --> 00:04:15.430
I watched it, I watched his face as he was
[makes gestures of restlessly looking around].

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:19.350
I was just a space holder.

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:25.880
And sure enough, somebody came in that he
knew or that he started to chat up and he

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:30.360
dropped me as though he had never seen me
before in his life, he didn’t even say “Lovely

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:31.360
talking with you.

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:33.710
My pal is here.”

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:37.210
And I watched him like a scientist because
I could see it happening.

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:44.950
Maybe it was my father’s sort of recent
lack of enthusiasm for my job that got me

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:50.139
primed for this but I watched this guy (as
I said) like a scientist.

00:04:50.139 --> 00:05:04.600
And I thought the casual rudeness and disregard
that I saw and still see is really, well it’s

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:07.550
no longer surprising but it is remarkable.

00:05:07.550 --> 00:05:17.660
It’s worthy of remarking on because it has
nothing to do with you or me or whoever is

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:23.570
the recipient of the rudeness, it says everything
about the person who hasn’t any social grace

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:29.139
or any experience in sharing life.

00:05:29.139 --> 00:05:40.700
Film Courage: Well, I think there is almost
an acceptable level of that here, where people

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:42.100
need to size you up “Okay, what can you
do for me?

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:46.389
Okay, this person can’t do anything for
me, I’m moving on.”

00:05:46.389 --> 00:05:49.550
I want to go back to the parent thing [Robin
spoke of previously].

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:54.610
I’ve seen two types of performers here and
those are people who have (and I wouldn’t

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:59.460
call them stage parents) but parents who just
follow everything they do and they are their

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:00.590
biggest supporters of them.

00:06:00.590 --> 00:06:02.520
And then I’ve seen the exact opposite.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:04.400
I don’t actually see a lot of grey area.

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:07.600
Either extreme support or “Oh, that’s
nice dear.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:08.600
Okay…”

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:09.960
And they change the subject.

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:15.240
And I think it’s interesting that you were
there to take care of yourself, to celebrate

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:17.290
for you.

00:06:17.290 --> 00:06:22.100
What would you say to the performers that
don’t have the parent who is the “cheerleader?”

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:24.110
Robin Riker: Get your tribe!

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:32.230
Find your people, make your family and stick
with them and support them and let them support

00:06:32.230 --> 00:06:33.640
you.

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:35.169
And take care of yourself.

00:06:35.169 --> 00:06:39.400
Make sure that you support you.

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:44.760
Because very rarely are members of the same
family conceived or born under the same roof.

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:49.320
You know, haven’t you noticed that you’ve
collected family, that your friends are the

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:52.419
family that you choose?

00:06:52.419 --> 00:06:55.040
And so it’s nice.

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:58.860
I know everybody wants their parent’s approval,
that’s really important.

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:05.699
I mean you can see all kind of psychological
damage done to children who are now grown

00:07:05.699 --> 00:07:11.480
adults who are still struggling for the love
of their parent or approval of their father

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:15.169
or mother or something, it’s a struggle.

00:07:15.169 --> 00:07:23.110
But be there for you and allow yourself to
celebrate.

00:07:23.110 --> 00:07:25.620
Other people don’t have to celebrate you.

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:31.449
You can celebrate yourself and it’s not
big-headed or anything.

00:07:31.449 --> 00:07:34.490
Let the Universe know “Thank you!”

00:07:34.490 --> 00:07:38.449
Something good has happened, this is terrific.

00:07:38.449 --> 00:07:44.710
And even if you are celebrating it by yourself
with a taco or a Horchata or a glass of champagne

00:07:44.710 --> 00:07:45.910
or meeting somebody.

00:07:45.910 --> 00:07:46.960
“Let’s go to the movies.”

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:49.320
Or “Let’s go for a walk on the beach or
fly a kite.”

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:52.710
Or whatever, but do it!

00:07:52.710 --> 00:08:00.150
Because we let all of these moments go by,
all of these celebratory moments.

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:02.820
And then we think we’ve just been living
this kind of life [makes a flatline motion

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:03.820
with her hands].

00:08:03.820 --> 00:08:06.210
When we really could have been living this
kind of life [hand raises up]…and this kind

00:08:06.210 --> 00:08:09.979
of life…then this kind of life [hand goes
down].

00:08:09.979 --> 00:08:16.120
You know, we have to acknowledge the moments,
if you don’t acknowledge it, I think it’s

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:19.810
with any kind of relationship (a human relationship,
you know?), if someone does something for

00:08:19.810 --> 00:08:25.960
you, and you acknowledge it, it increases
by many fold, that they will do another nice

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:27.390
thing for you.

00:08:27.390 --> 00:08:30.870
So if the Universe is doing nice things for
you and you go “Muh?

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:31.870
Hhmmm…”

00:08:31.870 --> 00:08:36.580
Don’t hurt the Universe’s feelings for
goodness sake!

00:08:36.580 --> 00:08:44.380
You need to acknowledge, it encourages, it
stimulates the flow of more good things to

00:08:44.380 --> 00:08:45.380
celebrate.

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:50.010
Film Courage: I feel like people don’t do
this until a major upset or death happens.

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:51.670
And I don’t mean to get so morbid.

00:08:51.670 --> 00:08:54.510
Robin Riker: I know what you mean, it’s
so true.

00:08:54.510 --> 00:09:00.570
It’s those moments in life that call everything
into question “Oh my Gosh!

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:05.040
Now I look around and all these passings that
we’ve had in the last year of these musicians

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:09.630
who are in their late 50’s or 60 years old
or something?

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:11.200
Way too young to go.

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:17.330
I can remember when I was 14, my boyfriend
and I and his mother drove down to Connecticut

00:09:17.330 --> 00:09:19.200
to see his grandfather.

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:23.089
And they were sitting around the table and
my boyfriend asked “How is Mr. Smith down

00:09:23.089 --> 00:09:24.089
the road?”

00:09:24.089 --> 00:09:25.520
And they said “Oh he passed away.

00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:26.520
Only 70.

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:28.100
Such a young man!”

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:34.600
And up until that moment, I had never thought
of life or age as relative.

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:38.690
I thought of it as whatever the opposite of
relative is.

00:09:38.690 --> 00:09:43.570
I’m 14, 70…that’s a million years old!

00:09:43.570 --> 00:09:50.950
But suddenly I saw this man who himself was
only age 69 or something, look at 70 as being

00:09:50.950 --> 00:09:51.950
young.

00:09:51.950 --> 00:09:55.570
And then I realized “Oh my Gosh!

00:09:55.570 --> 00:09:56.570
It is.”

00:09:56.570 --> 00:10:03.220
Why are we waiting to realize that life is
worth living right now?

00:10:03.220 --> 00:10:08.070
Why do we need Mr. Smith down the road to
pass away at the young age of 70 for us to

00:10:08.070 --> 00:10:12.800
start thinking about the days that we have?

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:21.660
Because you know what, (and this is not morbid,
this is just the truth) no one knows when

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:24.020
their time has come, you know?

00:10:24.020 --> 00:10:29.529
So if you keep waiting to have your adventures
or to celebrate…Oh!

00:10:29.529 --> 00:10:33.030
I have a really fun one to share with you…[to
be continued].

